ion** Occurs when the wound edges are closely approximated as in a clean surgical incision Features • Minimal tissue loss • Minimal granulation tissue • Faster epithelialization • Less scar formation • Better cosmetic result A clean uncomplicated surgical wound is the typical example **B Healing by Secondary Intention** Occurs when wound edges cannot be approximated usually because of significant tissue loss contamination or infection Features • Large amount of granulation tissue • Greater wound contraction • More extensive scar formation • Longer healing time • Higher risk of infection **C Healing by Tertiary Intention** Also called delayed primary closure The wound is initially left open because of contamination or infection and is closed surgically after the wound is clean and suitable for closure --- **2 Phases of Wound Healing** **Phase 1 Hemostasis** Time Begins immediately after injury **Main events** • Blood vessels initially constrict • Platelets adhere and aggregate at the injured site • Coagulation is activated • A fibrin clot forms and stops bleeding • The clot provides a temporary framework for cell migration • Platelets release growth factors and cytokines that initiate subsequent healing **Main purpose** To stop bleeding and provide a temporary matrix for repair --- **Phase 2 Inflammation** Time Begins shortly after injury and usually lasts several days **Main events** • Vasodilatation occurs • Vascular permeability increases • Neutrophils migrate into the wound • Monocytes enter and differentiate into macrophages • Dead tissue bacteria and cellular debris are removed by phagocytosis • Cytokines and growth factors are released to regulate further healing **Clinical signs** • Redness • Heat • Swelling • Pain • Exudate Important A controlled inflammatory response is essential but prolonged inflammation can delay healing --- **Phase 3 Proliferation** Time Begins within days and may continue for several weeks This phase involves **A Granulation tissue formation** • Fibroblasts proliferate • New extracellular matrix is produced • New capillaries develop **B Angiogenesis** Formation of new blood vessels provides oxygen and nutrients required for tissue repair **C Fibroplasia and collagen formation** Fibroblasts produce collagen and other extracellular matrix components **D Re epithelialization** • Keratinocytes migrate from the wound edges • New epithelium covers the wound surface **E Wound contraction** • Myofibroblasts contract the wound • Wound size progressively decreases **Main purpose** To fill the wound with new tissue and restore surface coverage --- **Phase 4 Remodeling / Maturation** Time Begins approximately around 3 weeks and may continue for months to about a year or longer **Main events** • Excess collagen is degraded • Type III collagen is progressively replaced/remodeled into stronger more organized type I collagen • Collagen fibers become more organized • Vascularity and cellularity decrease • Scar tissue becomes flatter and stronger • Tensile strength progressively increases **Important point** A healed wound does not regain 100 percent of the original tissue strength Maximum tensile strength is generally around 80 percent of normal tissue --- **3 Factors Affecting Wound Healing** **A Local factors** 1 Infection • Increases inflammation and delays healing 2 Poor blood supply • Reduces oxygen and nutrient delivery 3 Hypoxia • Impairs collagen synthesis and tissue repair 4 Excessive wound tension • May cause wound separation 5 Foreign bodies • Can maintain inflammation 6 Repeated trauma or pressure • Delays tissue repair 7 Excessive edema • Can impair oxygen diffusion and tissue nutrition **B Systemic factors** 1 Age 2 Malnutrition 3 Protein deficiency 4 Vitamin and mineral deficiencies 5 Diabetes mellitus 6 Anemia 7 Smoking 8 Immunosuppression 9 Poor circulation 10 Certain medications particularly corticosteroids 11 Systemic infection Adequate oxygenation nutrition and vascular supply are particularly important for normal healing --- **4 Complications of Wound Healing** **A Delayed healing** The wound takes longer than expected to heal **B Wound infection** May result in • Increased pain • Redness • Swelling • Purulent discharge • Fever **C Wound dehiscence** Separation of previously closed wound edges **D Ulceration** Failure of the wound to progress toward normal healing **E Excessive scar formation** • Hypertrophic scar • Keloid **F Contracture** Excessive contraction of scar tissue may restrict movement particularly around joints **G Chronic wound** Failure of the normal healing process can result in a persistent non healing wound --- **5 Physiotherapy Implications in Wound Healing** This is particularly important in a physiotherapy surgery examination **A Assessment** The physiotherapist should assess • Location and size of wound • Wound appearance • Swelling/edema • Pain • Skin condition • Range of motion • Muscle strength • Functional mobility • Signs of infection • Surgical precautions • Patients general medical condition --- **B Physiotherapy Management** 1 Positioning • Position the patient appropriately to reduce pressure on the wound • Elevate an affected limb when clinically appropriate to reduce dependent edema • Use pressure relieving positions in immobile patients 2 Prevention of pressure • Regular repositioning • Pressure relieving surfaces when indicated • Avoid prolonged pressure over healing tissue 3 Maintain joint mobility • Active ROM exercises • Active assisted exercises when required • Passive ROM for patients unable to move independently This helps prevent • Joint stiffness • Contractures • Loss of function 4 Early mobilization When medically and surgically appropriate • Bed mobility • Sitting • Standing • Transfers • Walking Early mobility helps prevent complications associated with prolonged immobilization and supports restoration of function 5 Edema management Depending on wound status and medical advice • Limb elevation • Active muscle pump exercises • Appropriate compression therapy when the wound and vascular status permit Compression should not be applied indiscriminately over an acute or infected wound 6 Muscle strengthening Once medically appropriate • Isometric exercises initially when indicated • Active strengthening • Progressive resistance exercises • Functional strengthening 7 Scar management After adequate wound closure and healing • Scar mobilization/manual techniques when appropriate • Stretching around scar tissue • ROM exercises • Functional activities The goal is to prevent adhesions restricted mobility and contractures 8 Respiratory physiotherapy For post operative patients at risk of respiratory complications • Breathing exercises • Thoracic expansion exercises • Supported coughing • Airway clearance when clinically indicated • Early mobilization 9 Functional rehabilitation • Transfer training • Gait training • Balance training • ADL training • Progressive endurance exercises • Return to work and recreational activities --- **6 Precautions for Physiotherapists** Physiotherapy should be modified when there is • Active wound infection • Wound dehiscence • Excessive bleeding • Uncontrolled pain • Significant wound discharge • Severe swelling • Unstable vital signs • Suspected DVT or systemic infection Avoid • Direct aggressive pressure over an unstable wound • Vigorous massage over infected tissue • Excessive stretching that places stress on a healing surgical wound • Exercise beyond the surgeons prescribed restrictions Any unexpected deterioration in wound condition should be reported to the appropriate medical/wound care team --- **7 Physiotherapy Goals** The major goals are 1 Promote safe functional recovery 2 Maintain ROM 3 Prevent contractures 4 Maintain muscle strength 5 Reduce complications of immobility 6 Control edema where appropriate 7 Improve mobility and gait 8 Prevent scar related functional restriction 9 Improve independence in ADLs 10 Educate the patient regarding wound protection and activity progression --- **Flowchart for Examination** Tissue injury Hemostasis Vasoconstriction platelet aggregation fibrin clot Inflammation Neutrophils macrophages removal of bacteria/debris Proliferation Fibroblasts collagen granulation tissue angiogenesis epithelialization contraction Remodeling/Maturation Collagen reorganization scar maturation increased tensile strength /////////////////////////////","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm considering the best way to approach problem-solving using available tools. Specifically, I'm now focused on tool selection. My current thinking prioritizes using the most specific tool for a given task, such as a file viewer instead of a general terminal. I'm exploring how this impacts efficiency and accuracy in problem-solving.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Application**\n\nI'm now deeply immersed in refining tool application based on very specific rules. Recalling the critical instructions, I am focusing on eliminating potentially inefficient tool usage. I'm actively avoiding `cat` for file creation/appending, preferring dedicated tools. I'm prioritizing `grep_search` over `grep` within bash and am working to avoid using `ls`, `cat`, `grep`, or `sed` for common tasks. My thought process now explicitly outlines all related tools before execution, ensuring the selection of the most specific, efficient option.\n\n\n**Structuring Thought Processing**\n\nI'm now focusing on structuring the thought processing itself based on new guidelines 1 I'm incorporating rules about punctuation and formatting to improve clarity and organization -- I'm currently working to integrate these guidelines into my thought process to avoid non-alphanumeric punctuation and excessive line breaks • I'm carefully evaluating how these changes impact the flow and accessibility of the generated output • My current goal is to efficiently adhere to these constraints, ensuring the process is both compliant and logical /////////////\n\n\n