nger strings, transforming concatenated sections to use appropriate spacing and formatting, converting \"—\" to spaces. I'm focusing on ensuring uniformity across the entire text. I'm also ensuring that sections are clearly delineated.\n\n\n**Organizing and Prioritizing**\n\nI'm now organizing the provided medical topics and prioritizing them effectively. I've structured the content, including \"Wound infection,\" \"Atelectasis,\" and other conditions into a well-defined list format. I'm focusing on ensuring clear hierarchy and accurate representation. I am managing the formatting of headings and subheadings.\n\n\n**Structuring and Prioritizing**\n\nI'm now integrating the current topics into a cohesive, organized format. I've classified and prioritized medical topics such as surgical infections, fluid balance, and various surgical procedures. I'm focusing on \"Sutures,\" \"Surgical infections,\" and related topics, organizing the listed content under the appropriate headers and subheaders, ensuring proper presentation and expanding abbreviations, such as the change from `---` to \"---\".\n\n\n**Categorizing & Summarizing**\n\nI'm now focusing on categorizing and summarizing the syllabus content into organized sections. I've categorized radiology, anesthesia, and other key medical topics. In the 'If You Have VERY LITTLE TIME' section, I have transformed it into a numbered list, while continuing to follow established formatting guidelines. I'm focusing on ensuring uniformity, expanding abbreviations, and maintaining clear structure.\n\n\n**Refining Topic Organization**\n\nI'm now refining the organization of medical topics and ensuring consistent formatting. I'm focusing on \"Postoperative complications,\" \"PAOD,\" and similar entries, expanding \"VT\" as required. I have resolved issues with \"—\" and ensured spacing and hyphenation are correct, for example, \"SURGERY I MOST IMPORTANT TOPICS\". I have also handled \"18% / 10 hrs\" and removed extraneous brackets, changing \"Deep Brain Stimulation (DBS)\" to \"Deep Brain Stimulation DBS\". I am also handling multiple topics in one statement, and converting the directional arrow.\n\n\n**Preserving Importance Weights**\n\nI'm now focused on preserving the weighting of medical topics after removing stars (⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐). I've changed the star ratings into spelled-out values: \"5 stars,\" \"4 stars,\" and \"3 stars.\" For example, \"Postoperative complications and physiotherapy 5 stars,\" which reflects the initial assessment. I am managing the formatting and making sure this does not violate any rules.\n\n\n**Finalizing Prioritization Rules**\n\nI am now completing the prioritization, confirming the string's integrity. I converted the star ratings into their textual representations: \"5 stars,\" \"4 stars,\" and \"3 stars.\" I also applied the conversion rules to the string containing medical topics, transforming all remaining symbols. I have conducted a thorough review of the formatted data.\n\n\n**Organizing Medical Content**\n\nI am now organizing the neurosurgery and infection content provided. I've structured topics like craniectomy, laminectomy, and aneurysm procedures, and grouped them with \"UNIT 2\" and \"UNIT 3\" topics. I am correctly using the three hyphens. I am ensuring that the textual representations of numerical values are correct, and making sure that all topics are listed in a clear and organized fashion.\n\n\n**Structuring Medical Content**\n\nI'm now arranging the oncology, amputation, and vascular content with a clear structure. I've categorized mastectomy, amputation procedures, varicose veins, and PAOD, including the relevant subtopics under the appropriate headers. I'm ensuring proper format. The conversion of percentages into textual format has been handled correctly. I'm focusing on \"UNIT 1 SURGICAL PRINCIPLES\" to ensure its proper format.\n\n\n**Categorizing Surgical Procedures**\n\nI'm now cataloging surgical procedures, prioritizing critical content from the syllabus. I've integrated \"UNIT 4 ABDOMINAL SURGERY\", and expanded on the topics including appendectomy, herniorrhaphy, and cholecystectomy, which will be organized as I review the other topics. I am also working on converting \"8 percent\" to \"8 percent\" and \"12 percent\" into \"12 percent\" throughout the content.\n\n\n**Finalizing Syllabus Structure**\n\nI'm now integrating all medical topics to form the final syllabus structure, focusing on consistent formatting and clear hierarchies. I've correctly integrated the provided content on \"Endsem\", ensuring that I have numbered each topic. I am converting all the percentage values to their textual form. Finally, I will ensure the formatting for all bullet points.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":7,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T08:43:56Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nAbdominal Surgery: Incisions, Common Procedures and Physiotherapy Implications—-\r\n\r\nDefinition of Abdominal Surgery\r\n\r\nAbdominal surgery refers to surgical procedures performed to diagnose, treat, repair, remove or reconstruct structures within the abdominal and pelvic cavities, including the gastrointestinal, hepatobiliary, urinary and reproductive organs.\r\n\r\nAn open abdominal operation (laparotomy) involves an incision through the abdominal wall to access the abdominal cavity, whereas laparoscopic surgery uses several small incisions and a camera. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. Common Abdominal Incisions\r\n\r\nThe choice of incision depends on the organ involved, required surgical exposure, urgency of surgery and patient factors. \r\n\r\nA. Midline incision\r\n\r\n* Vertical incision through the linea alba.\r\n* May be upper, lower or full midline.\r\n* Provides rapid and wide access to the abdominal cavity.\r\n* Commonly used in emergency and exploratory laparotomy.\r\n* Easily extended if greater exposure is required. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy relevance:\r\nLarge midline wounds may cause pain during coughing, deep breathing and movement. Patients require wound support, breathing exercises and gradual mobilization.\r\n\r\nB. Paramedian incision\r\n\r\n* Vertical incision placed lateral to the midline.\r\n* May provide access to selected abdominal organs.\r\n* Less commonly used today than the midline approach. \r\n\r\nC. Transverse incision\r\n\r\n* Horizontal incision across the abdominal wall.\r\n* May provide good cosmetic results and can follow the direction of abdominal wall structures.\r\n* Used for selected abdominal procedures. \r\n\r\nD. Pfannenstiel incision\r\n\r\n* Curved transverse incision just above the pubic symphysis.\r\n* Commonly used for pelvic and gynaecological surgery, including abdominal hysterectomy.\r\n* Provides good cosmetic results and relatively limited upper-abdominal access. \r\n\r\nE. Subcostal / Kocher incision\r\n\r\n* Oblique incision below and parallel to the costal margin.\r\n* Right subcostal: commonly used for gallbladder and liver surgery.\r\n* Left subcostal: may be used for splenic surgery.\r\n* Bilateral subcostal incisions joined in the midline are called a rooftop incision. \r\n\r\nF. McBurney / Grid-iron incision\r\n\r\n* Oblique muscle-splitting incision in the right lower quadrant.\r\n* Traditionally associated with open appendectomy. \r\n\r\nG. Lanz / transverse right lower-quadrant incision\r\n\r\n* Transverse incision in the right lower abdomen.\r\n* May be used for open appendectomy.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. General Physiotherapy Implications of Abdominal Surgery\r\n\r\nAbdominal surgery can produce pain, reduced ventilation, impaired coughing, reduced mobility, muscle weakness and risk of postoperative complications. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy assessment\r\n\r\n* Vital signs\r\n* Respiratory rate and SpO₂\r\n* Breathing pattern\r\n* Chest expansion\r\n* Cough and sputum\r\n* Pain\r\n* Wound and surgical precautions\r\n* Abdominal distension\r\n* ROM and muscle strength\r\n* Bed mobility and functional mobility\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy management\r\n\r\nA. Respiratory physiotherapy\r\n\r\n* Deep breathing exercises\r\n* Thoracic expansion exercises\r\n* Supported coughing\r\n* Airway-clearance techniques when clinically indicated\r\n* Appropriate positioning\r\n\r\nB. Early mobilization\r\n\r\n* Bed mobility\r\n* Sitting out of bed\r\n* Sit-to-stand\r\n* Transfers\r\n* Progressive walking\r\n\r\nEarly mobilization helps reduce complications associated with immobility and supports recovery. \r\n\r\nC. Circulatory exercises\r\n\r\n* Ankle pumps\r\n* Active limb exercises\r\n* Early ambulation\r\n\r\nThese help reduce venous stasis and risk of thromboembolic complications.\r\n\r\nD. Abdominal wound protection\r\n\r\n* Teach log rolling for getting in and out of bed.\r\n* Support the incision during coughing when appropriate.\r\n* Avoid excessive strain on the healing abdominal wall.\r\n* Progress activity according to surgical restrictions.\r\n\r\nE. Musculoskeletal rehabilitation\r\n\r\n* ROM exercises\r\n* Progressive strengthening\r\n* Postural exercises\r\n* Gait and functional training\r\n* Gradual return to activities of daily living.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Common Abdominal and Pelvic Surgical Procedures\r\n\r\nA. Herniorrhaphy\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nHerniorrhaphy is the surgical repair of a hernia by returning the protruding tissue to its normal position and repairing/closing the defect in the abdominal wall.\r\n\r\nCommon types\r\n\r\n* Inguinal hernia\r\n* Femoral hernia\r\n* Umbilical hernia\r\n* Incisional hernia\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Pain management through positioning and activity modification.\r\n* Breathing exercises and supported coughing.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Gentle ROM exercises.\r\n* Progressive walking.\r\n* Gradual abdominal and trunk strengthening after adequate healing and according to surgical instructions.\r\n* Avoid excessive straining or heavy lifting until medically cleared.\r\n* Patient education regarding safe body mechanics.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Colostomy\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nA colostomy is a surgical procedure in which a portion of the colon is brought through an opening in the abdominal wall to form a stoma, allowing feces to pass into an external stoma bag. \r\n\r\nTypes\r\n\r\n* Loop colostomy\r\n* End colostomy\r\n\r\nA colostomy may be temporary or permanent depending on the underlying condition. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Respiratory exercises after surgery.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Avoid direct pressure on the stoma.\r\n* Teach safe bed mobility and transfers.\r\n* Gradual strengthening.\r\n* Improve posture and functional mobility.\r\n* Educate regarding protection of the stoma during exercise.\r\n* Avoid excessive abdominal pressure during the early healing period.\r\n* Coordinate with the stoma-care team regarding appliances and activity.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Ileostomy\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nAn ileostomy is a surgical procedure in which the ileum, the terminal part of the small intestine, is brought through the abdominal wall to form a stoma. \r\n\r\nTypes\r\n\r\n* Loop ileostomy\r\n* End ileostomy\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Breathing exercises.\r\n* ROM and circulation exercises.\r\n* Protect the stoma during transfers and exercise.\r\n* Avoid excessive abdominal pressure during early healing.\r\n* Gradual strengthening and aerobic activity.\r\n* Monitor fatigue, hydration status and exercise tolerance because ileostomy patients can be susceptible to fluid and electrolyte losses.\r\n* Patient education regarding safe activity and stoma protection.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. Hysterectomy\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nHysterectomy is the surgical removal of the uterus (womb). Depending on the procedure, the cervix, fallopian tubes and ovaries may also be removed. \r\n\r\nTypes\r\n\r\n1. Total hysterectomy\r\n2. Subtotal/supracervical hysterectomy\r\n3. Total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy\r\n4. Radical hysterectomy\r\n\r\nIt may be performed abdominally, vaginally or laparoscopically. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Breathing exercises.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Circulatory exercises.\r\n* Gentle ROM.\r\n* Postural correction.\r\n* Gradual abdominal and pelvic rehabilitation.\r\n* Pelvic floor muscle training when appropriate.\r\n* Gait and functional training.\r\n* Avoid heavy lifting until adequate healing and medical clearance.\r\n* Education regarding safe return to daily activities.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n7. Prostatectomy\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nProstatectomy is the surgical removal of part or all of the prostate gland.\r\n\r\nIt may be performed for conditions such as prostate cancer or severe benign prostatic enlargement, depending on the surgical approach.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Breathing and circulation exercises.\r\n* ROM exercises.\r\n* Gait training.\r\n* Pelvic floor muscle training to help manage postoperative urinary incontinence when appropriate.\r\n* Functional strengthening.\r\n* Patient education regarding activity progression.\r\n* Avoid excessive strain during early recovery.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n8. Cystectomy\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nCystectomy is the surgical removal of the urinary bladder, most commonly performed as treatment for selected cases of bladder cancer.\r\n\r\nA urinary diversion is generally required after radical cystectomy.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Respiratory exercises.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Prevention of DVT and complications of immobility.\r\n* ROM and strengthening.\r\n* Gait training.\r\n* Functional rehabilitation.\r\n* Appropriate pelvic floor rehabilitation where indicated.\r\n* Education regarding safe movement with urinary drainage systems or stoma appliances.\r\n* Monitor fatigue and exercise tolerance.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n9. Appendectomy\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nAppendectomy is the surgical removal of the vermiform appendix, most commonly performed for acute appendicitis.\r\n\r\nIt may be performed by:\r\n\r\n* Open surgery\r\n* Laparoscopic surgery\r\n\r\nThe traditional open approach may use a McBurney/Grid-iron incision or a transverse right lower-quadrant incision. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Deep breathing exercises.\r\n* Supported coughing.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Gentle ROM.\r\n* Gradual walking.\r\n* Pain-relieving positioning.\r\n* Wound protection.\r\n* Progressive return to normal activities after uncomplicated healing.\r\n\r\nIf perforation or peritonitis has occurred, rehabilitation may need to progress more slowly because of greater systemic illness and surgical complexity.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n10. Cholecystectomy\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nCholecystectomy is the surgical removal of the gallbladder, commonly performed for symptomatic gallstones and related gallbladder disease.\r\n\r\nApproaches\r\n\r\n* Laparoscopic cholecystectomy\r\n* Open cholecystectomy\r\n\r\nOpen surgery may use a right subcostal/Kocher incision. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Deep breathing and thoracic expansion exercises.\r\n* Supported coughing.\r\n* Appropriate positioning.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Circulatory exercises.\r\n* ROM exercises.\r\n* Gradual gait and endurance training.\r\n* Protect the abdominal wound.\r\n* Progressive return to ADLs.\r\n\r\nUpper abdominal incisions can be particularly uncomfortable during deep inspiration and coughing, so respiratory rehabilitation is important./////////////////////                                                                                  2 Marks Each\r\n\r\n(a) DVT: Signs and Prophylaxis\r\n\r\nSigns of DVT\r\n\r\n* Calf pain and tenderness.\r\n* Unilateral swelling of the leg.\r\n* Redness and warmth.\r\n* Increased calf circumference.\r\n* Homan’s sign may be positive, but it is not reliable for diagnosis.\r\n\r\nProphylaxis\r\n\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Ankle-pump exercises.\r\n* Adequate hydration.\r\n* Graduated compression stockings when indicated.\r\n* Intermittent pneumatic compression.\r\n* Anticoagulants such as LMWH when prescribed.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n(b) Scar: Definition and Types\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Scar: Fibrous tissue formed during healing that replaces normal tissue after injury.\r\n\r\nTypes\r\n\r\n1. Normal scar\r\n    * Flat and soft.\r\n    * Example: healed surgical incision.\r\n2. Hypertrophic scar\r\n    * Raised and thick.\r\n    * Remains within the original wound margins.\r\n    * Example: scar following burns.\r\n3. Keloid scar\r\n    * Excessive raised scar tissue.\r\n    * Extends beyond the original wound margins.\r\n    * Example: scar after ear piercing.\r\n4. Atrophic scar\r\n    * Thin and depressed.\r\n    * Example: acne or chickenpox scars.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n(c) Classification of Antibiotics\r\n\r\n1. Cell-wall synthesis inhibitors\r\n\r\n* Penicillins.\r\n* Cephalosporins.\r\n* Carbapenems.\r\n* Vancomycin.\r\n\r\n2. Protein-synthesis inhibitors\r\n\r\n* Aminoglycosides.\r\n* Macrolides.\r\n* Tetracyclines.\r\n* Clindamycin.\r\n\r\n3. Nucleic-acid synthesis inhibitors\r\n\r\n* Fluoroquinolones.\r\n* Rifampicin.\r\n* Metronidazole.\r\n\r\n4. Antimetabolites\r\n\r\n* Sulfonamides.\r\n* Trimethoprim.\r\n\r\n5. Cell-membrane disruptors\r\n\r\n* Polymyxins.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n(d) Sip Feeding vs Tube Feeding\r\n\r\nSip Feeding\r\n\r\n* Feeding is given orally in small sips.\r\n* Requires adequate swallowing ability.\r\n* Patient actively participates in feeding.\r\n* Suitable for patients who can swallow safely.\r\n* Examples: water, milk and oral nutritional supplements.\r\n\r\nTube Feeding\r\n\r\n* Nutrition is given through a feeding tube.\r\n* Used when oral feeding is unsafe or inadequate.\r\n* Does not require normal oral swallowing for delivery.\r\n* Examples:\r\n    * Nasogastric (NG) tube.\r\n    * Gastrostomy/PEG tube.\r\n    * Jejunostomy tube.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n(e) Cardiovascular Parameters Monitored During Anesthesia\r\n\r\n* Heart rate (HR).\r\n* Blood pressure (BP).\r\n* ECG and cardiac rhythm.\r\n* Oxygen saturation (SpO₂).\r\n* Peripheral perfusion.\r\n* Urine output when indicated.\r\n* Temperature may also be monitored perioperatively.\r\n\r\nImportant for 2 marks:\r\n\r\nHR + BP + ECG + SpO₂\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n(f) Loss of Water: Clinical Manifestations and Treatment\r\n\r\nClinical Manifestations\r\n\r\n* Thirst.\r\n* Dry mouth.\r\n* Dry mucous membranes.\r\n* Reduced urine output.\r\n* Dark/concentrated urine.\r\n* Weakness.\r\n* Fatigue.\r\n* Dizziness.\r\n* Tachycardia.\r\n* Hypotension.\r\n* Poor skin turgor.\r\n* Confusion in severe dehydration.\r\n\r\nTreatment\r\n\r\n* Oral rehydration solution (ORS) for suitable mild/moderate cases.\r\n* IV fluids for severe dehydration or shock.\r\n* Replace ongoing fluid losses.\r\n* Correct electrolyte abnormalities.\r\n* Treat the underlying cause.\r\n* Monitor:\r\n    * Fluid intake/output.\r\n    * Blood pressure.\r\n    * Heart rate.\r\n    * Urine output.\r\n    * Serum electrolytes.////////////////////////////////                                                                                                          Compare CT and MRI in Evaluation of Brain Lesions—\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nCT Scan\r\n\r\n* Computed Tomography (CT) is an imaging technique that uses X-rays and computer processing to produce cross-sectional images of the brain.\r\n* It is fast and widely available.\r\n* It is particularly useful in acute emergencies, especially suspected intracranial hemorrhage and head trauma.\r\n\r\nMRI\r\n\r\n* Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is an imaging technique that uses a strong magnetic field and radiofrequency waves to produce detailed images of the brain.\r\n* It provides superior soft-tissue contrast compared with CT.\r\n* It is particularly useful for detailed assessment of brain lesions.\r\n* CT: Advantages in Brain Lesions\r\n\r\n* Rapid examination.\r\n* Excellent for acute intracranial hemorrhage.\r\n* Excellent for skull fractures.\r\n* Useful in head injury.\r\n* Useful for detecting mass effect and hydrocephalus.\r\n* Useful in emergency situations.\r\n* More suitable for patients who cannot tolerate prolonged imaging.\r\n\r\nCT: Limitations\r\n\r\n* Uses ionizing radiation.\r\n* Lower soft-tissue contrast than MRI.\r\n* Small or subtle lesions may be less conspicuous.\r\n* Posterior fossa imaging can be affected by bone artifacts.\r\n* Iodinated contrast may cause allergic reactions or contrast-associated complications in susceptible patients.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nMRI: Advantages in Brain Lesions\r\n\r\n* Provides excellent soft-tissue contrast.\r\n* Better for characterization of brain tumors.\r\n* Highly sensitive for ischemic stroke, particularly with diffusion-weighted imaging.\r\n* Better visualization of:\r\n    * Brainstem.\r\n    * Cerebellum.\r\n    * Posterior fossa.\r\n    * White matter.\r\n    * Cranial nerves.\r\n* Useful for detecting:\r\n    * Demyelinating lesions.\r\n    * Brain tumors.\r\n    * Inflammatory lesions.\r\n    * Small infarcts.\r\n    * Some infections.\r\n* No ionizing radiation.\r\n\r\nMRI: Limitations\r\n\r\n* Longer examination time.\r\n* More expensive.\r\n* Less available in some settings.\r\n* Sensitive to patient movement.\r\n* May be difficult for claustrophobic patients.\r\n* Some implanted medical devices or metallic foreign bodies may make MRI unsafe or require special precautions.\r\n* Monitoring critically ill patients can be more difficult.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nClinical Selection\r\n\r\nCT is preferred when:\r\n\r\n* Acute head injury is suspected.\r\n* Intracranial hemorrhage is suspected.\r\n* Rapid emergency imaging is required.\r\n* Skull fracture needs assessment.\r\n* MRI is contraindicated or unavailable.\r\n\r\nMRI is preferred when:\r\n\r\n* Detailed characterization of a brain lesion is required.\r\n* Brain tumor is suspected.\r\n* Small or subtle lesions need evaluation.\r\n* Demyelinating disease is suspected.\r\n* Early ischemic stroke needs sensitive imaging.\r\n* Posterior fossa or brainstem pathology is suspected.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy Relevance\r\n\r\n* Imaging findings help physiotherapists understand the location and extent of neurological lesions.\r\n* CT may identify hemorrhage, mass effect or structural injury.\r\n* MRI can provide detailed information about areas responsible for:\r\n    * Motor function.\r\n    * Sensation.\r\n    * Coordination.\r\n    * Balance.\r\n    * Speech.\r\n* Physiotherapy assessment and treatment should be based on the patient’s clinical neurological findings, not imaging alone.\r\n* In acute brain lesions, monitor:\r\n    * Level of consciousness.\r\n    * Vital signs.\r\n    * Motor function.\r\n    * Tone.\r\n    * Sensation.\r\n    * Balance.\r\n    * Functional mobility.//////////////////////////                                                                                                       Postural Advice Following Abdominal\r\nDefinition—\r\n\r\n* Postural advice following abdominal surgery refers to teaching the patient safe positions and movement strategies that reduce pain, protect the surgical wound, improve breathing and circulation, and facilitate early functional recovery.\r\n\r\nAims of Postural Advice\r\n\r\n* Reduce pain and discomfort.\r\n* Protect the abdominal incision.\r\n* Improve lung expansion.\r\n* Facilitate effective coughing.\r\n* Reduce risk of pressure injuries.\r\n* Promote venous circulation.\r\n* Prevent stiffness and contractures.\r\n* Facilitate early mobilization and independence.\r\n\r\nPostural Advice\r\n\r\n1. Semi-Fowler’s position\r\n\r\n* Elevate the head and trunk approximately 30–45°.\r\n* Support the knees with a pillow if comfortable.\r\n* Reduces tension on the abdominal wound.\r\n* Facilitates diaphragmatic breathing.\r\n* Useful for resting and breathing exercises.\r\n\r\n2. Supine position\r\n\r\n* Keep the patient comfortably supported.\r\n* Place a pillow under the knees if required.\r\n* Avoid excessive abdominal stretching.\r\n* Change position regularly to prevent pressure-related complications.\r\n\r\n3. Side-lying position\r\n\r\n* Encourage side-lying when medically permitted.\r\n* Place a pillow between the knees for comfort.\r\n* Support the abdomen with a pillow if needed.\r\n* Helps vary pressure areas and may improve comfort.\r\n\r\n4. Sitting position\r\n\r\n* Encourage sitting upright as soon as medically safe.\r\n* Keep the trunk supported and avoid excessive slumping.\r\n* Feet should be supported on the floor or footrest.\r\n* Upright positioning assists ventilation and prepares the patient for mobilization.\r\n\r\n5. Standing and walking\r\n\r\n* Progress from sitting to standing when medically stable.\r\n* Maintain an upright posture.\r\n* Begin with short-distance walking.\r\n* Gradually increase walking distance and duration.\r\n* Avoid sudden twisting or excessive trunk strain.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nAdvice During Coughing\r\n\r\n* Teach the patient to support the abdominal incision with their hands or a pillow.\r\n* Take a deep breath before coughing.\r\n* Perform a controlled cough rather than repeated forceful coughing.\r\n* Splinting can reduce pain and improve confidence during coughing.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nAdvice for Getting Out of Bed\r\n\r\nLog-rolling technique\r\n\r\n1. Bend the knees.\r\n2. Roll onto the side as one unit.\r\n3. Keep the shoulders and pelvis moving together.\r\n4. Lower the legs over the side of the bed.\r\n5. Push up with the arms to sit.\r\n6. Avoid sudden abdominal flexion or twisting.\r\n7. Stand after achieving balance.\r\n\r\nBenefits\r\n\r\n* Reduces strain on the abdominal incision.\r\n* Minimizes pain.\r\n* Promotes safe independence.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nPrecautions\r\n\r\n* Avoid prolonged bed rest.\r\n* Avoid excessive abdominal stretching or twisting in the early healing period.\r\n* Avoid heavy lifting until medically cleared.\r\n* Avoid positions that significantly increase pain.\r\n* Change position regularly.\r\n* Follow the surgeon’s specific wound and activity restrictions.\r\n* Monitor for dizziness, breathlessness, wound pain or other deterioration during mobilization.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy Role\r\n\r\n* Assess posture, pain, breathing and mobility.\r\n* Teach appropriate resting positions.\r\n* Teach supported coughing.\r\n* Teach bed mobility and log rolling.\r\n* Encourage early mobilization.\r\n* Prescribe ROM and circulation exercises.\r\n* Progress walking and functional activities.\r\n* Educate the patient about safe posture and gradual return to normal activities.////////////////////////         Radical Hysterectomy\r\n\r\nIndications and Surgical - \r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure in which the uterus and cervix are removed along with the parametrial/paracervical tissues and a portion of the upper vagina, usually with pelvic lymph-node assessment or dissection.\r\n* It is primarily performed for selected cases of early invasive cervical cancer. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. Indications\r\n\r\n* Early invasive carcinoma of the cervix is the main indication.\r\n* Selected FIGO stage IA2 cervical cancer.\r\n* Selected stage IB cervical cancer.\r\n* Selected stage IIA cervical cancer, depending on tumour size, spread and patient factors.\r\n* Tumour confined to the cervix/upper vagina without disease that makes primary surgery unsuitable.\r\n* Selected patients with small, operable cervical tumours who are medically fit for major surgery.\r\n* Pelvic lymph-node assessment/dissection is commonly performed with radical hysterectomy. \r\n\r\nImportant point\r\n\r\n* Patients with bulky tumour or radiological lymph-node involvement are generally not suitable for primary radical hysterectomy and may be referred for chemoradiation or other appropriate treatment. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Structures Removed\r\n\r\nDuring radical hysterectomy, the surgeon generally removes:\r\n\r\n* Uterus.\r\n* Cervix.\r\n* Parametrial/paracervical tissues.\r\n* Upper portion of vagina.\r\n* Pelvic lymph nodes when lymphadenectomy is indicated.\r\n* Fallopian tubes and ovaries may or may not be removed, depending on age, tumour characteristics and clinical indication. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Surgical Steps\r\n\r\nPre-operative preparation\r\n\r\n* Patient is placed in the appropriate operative position.\r\n* General anesthesia is administered.\r\n* Urinary/Foley catheter is inserted.\r\n* Abdomen and vagina are prepared and draped.\r\n* Thromboembolism prophylaxis is provided as appropriate. \r\n\r\nStep 1: Abdominal entry\r\n\r\n* Abdomen is opened, traditionally through a vertical midline incision for open surgery.\r\n* Abdominal and pelvic organs are inspected for metastatic disease.\r\n* Suspicious lymph nodes or other lesions are assessed. \r\n\r\nStep 2: Exposure of the pelvis\r\n\r\n* Bowel is displaced from the pelvis.\